<Project>

  <!--
    Copy this file to `signing.props` (which is gitignored) on the machine that cuts Android
    releases, and fill in the two paths and the password.

    It holds a credential, so treat it exactly like the hub's .env: it is real on one machine
    and absent everywhere else. Absent, the csproj's conditional import does nothing and the
    build falls back to the Android SDK's debug keystore, which is what keeps `dotnet build`
    working for anyone who only wants to compile.

    ## Making the keystore

    There is no certificate authority in this story. An Android release key is self-signed and
    free, unlike the Windows code-signing certificate roadmap #11 is waiting on:

      keytool -genkeypair -v -keystore fleethub-agent.keystore -storetype PKCS12 ^
              -alias fleethub -keyalg RSA -keysize 4096 -validity 10000

    `-validity 10000` is about 27 years. An expired key cannot sign an update, and there is no
    renewal path that keeps the same identity, so err long.

    PKCS12 keeps ONE password for the store and the key, which is why both properties below
    take the same value. (JKS allowed them to differ; it is legacy and keytool warns about it.)

    ## Why this file is fleet state, not build configuration

    Losing this keystore costs two separate things, and neither is recoverable:

      * No installed agent can be updated in place. Android refuses an APK signed with a
        different key, so every device would need an uninstall by hand.
      * Every device's SSAID changes. This agent reports the SSAID as serial_number (see
        agent-android/README.md), and it is derived per app-signing key -- so the entire
        Android fleet would re-appear in the console as new machines at once, and the hub's
        duplicate-serial merge could not put them back together.

    So it belongs wherever BACKUP_MASTER_KEY is kept, and it is carried across a server
    migration before anything else.

    ## Passwords without a password on disk

    Both password properties accept an `env:` prefix, which reads an environment variable at
    build time instead:

      <AndroidSigningStorePass>env:FLEETHUB_KEYSTORE_PASS</AndroidSigningStorePass>

    Prefer that on any machine where this file would otherwise be the only plaintext copy.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Release'">
    <AndroidKeyStore>true</AndroidKeyStore>

    <!-- An absolute path. This file is imported from a csproj two directories down, so a
         relative one would resolve against the wrong place depending on how MSBuild was
         invoked. -->
    <AndroidSigningKeyStore>C:\path\to\fleethub-agent.keystore</AndroidSigningKeyStore>

    <!-- The -alias given to keytool above. -->
    <AndroidSigningKeyAlias>fleethub</AndroidSigningKeyAlias>

    <!-- The same value twice: PKCS12 has one password. Use env:NAME to read it from the
         environment instead of storing it here. -->
    <AndroidSigningStorePass>your-keystore-password</AndroidSigningStorePass>
    <AndroidSigningKeyPass>your-keystore-password</AndroidSigningKeyPass>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
